WEBVTT

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:01.940
Over there.

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:03.350
Yeah, over there.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.100
Next, over there.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.610
Yeah, over there.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.190
Then go around to the other side.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.780
Okay, got it. Like this?

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.320
She's really moving to the gardening club?

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.570
Is the cosplay club in danger of disbanding?

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.660
I don't think it's anything like that...

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.330
Watching those two

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.580
makes my insides feel kind of po-warm...

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.330
Po-warm?

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.090
Po-warm...

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.260
You're right.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.640
It's unusual for you to say things
like that, Nanako.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.010
Oh... Yeah.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:41.770
Po-warm...

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.020
A heart-warming state.
Additionally, the onoma-
topoeia for such a state.
Also "powarm."
A combination of
"poppo" (JPN)
and "warm" (ENG).
The reading resembles
tender emotions within
ones mental state.
A compound word.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.620
After-School Pleiades

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:05.790
Sobbingly, I went on my journey

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:10.250
Wishing I could at least fly through the sky

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.630
And yet the stars gently shone on me in the nights after our quarrels

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:22.180
I wonder if you were also watching them

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:27.440
I don't know... I want to know... I want to feel it...

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:34.490
Nothingness simply means there are infinite possibilities

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.280
The excitement I got after realizing that

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.370
was like surfing the waves of space and time

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.460
How strange that I'm not afraid anymore

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.170
Let's embrace our imperfections and go on

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.050
We'll open the promised door

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:57.220
and gain dazzling smiles!

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.990
Nanako 13

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.830
We've discovered a new fragment.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.410
The jellyfish's illumination is our meteor forecast?

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.960
This time, the fragment lies on the
outer edge of this solar system.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.630
Th-The outer edge?

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:34.210
Is that far away?

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:37.550
Yes, in terms of distance,
it's roughly 0.25 light-years away.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.590
About fifteen thousand times the distance
between Earth and the Sun.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.470
Fifteen thousand?! What the heck?

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.350
Our distance from Saturn is about
ten times our distance from the Sun.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:52.110
The farthest planet, Neptune,
is about thirty times farther.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.820
And this time, it's fifteen thousand times farther?

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.110
That's still inside our solar system?

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.490
Past Neptune lies the Edgeworth-Kuiper Belt.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.780
Beyond that lies the home
of comets, the Oort Cloud,

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.120
and near that is our next destination.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.910
Why is it all the way out there?

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.370
I don't know if the fragments
are getting farther away

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:15.170
or if our ability to detect them is improving...

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:18.010
We can't just open the window and be there?

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:20.760
Oh, right, like last time...

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.970
Unfortunately, we can't use that method this time.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:27.720
What about drawing it, or being drawn
to it, like we did with Saturn's?

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.270
Impossible. The distance is a bit too great.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.150
However, if one person were to reach it,
that would be a different story.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.320
If someone had a clear perception
of the fragment's location,

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.280
a connection would be instantly forged,

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.160
and we could leap 0.25 light-years at once.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.660
But how do we get that first person there?

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.240
If the five of you combine your powers,

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.040
launching one person out there is possible.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:56.090
But 0.25 light-years is a
quarter of a light-year, so...

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.800
So, um, at the speed of light,
that would be three months?

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:05.050
I can't make any of you skip school for that long...

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:11.230
I shall go.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:12.600
Huh?!

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.110
Whoa.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.860
That looks cool.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:31.000
The max speed of your improved drive
shafts is 90% of the speed of light.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:35.500
With all five together, you can reach 99.999%!

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.920
That's five 9s.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.380
Pioneer 12 or Voyager 3?

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.220
Which looks better?

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:44.550
She looks like she's having fun.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:45.720
She's really into it.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.600
Are you sure?

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.220
Yes. All of you have school.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.640
The same goes for you, doesn't it?

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.440
N-Nanako, I'll go with—

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.150
I'd like to go alone.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.480
We'll be setting out early tomorrow.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.950
Let's head home for today
and prepare for tomorrow.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:06.990
I'm home.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:08.490
Welcome home.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.170
Is the stew good?

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:17.210
Yes.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.460
Thank you.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:21.710
Oh, Nanako...

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:24.970
Do you have any requests for
dinner tomorrow night?

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.600
Anything is fine. Let me know.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.730
Buenas noches.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:50.160
Sabah el kheir.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.120
I'm off.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:07.340
He says this is Nanako's stand-in.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.760
Good morning! I'm off! I'm home!

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:12.010
Good morning! I'm off! I'm home!

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:13.810
It can only say set phrases?

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:16.230
Won't her family catch on to this?

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.560
No, it will work.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:18.980
It will?

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.310
What if the teacher calls on you in class?

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:23.070
Yes! I don't know!

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.320
Seriously?

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.440
It's fine. No one will notice.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:28.320
Yes! I don't know!

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:32.240
We're going to live on this for three months?

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.580
Oh, but the more closely she
approaches the speed of light,

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.830
the more slowly time moves for her, right?

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:38.830
Precisely!

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:42.540
If she flies at 99.999% of the speed of light,

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:47.340
then a quarter light-year will only
feel like half a day to Nanako.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.260
That short?

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:53.100
That's the biggest reason for traveling
close to the speed of light.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.470
Wow...

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:55.850
Oh, right!

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:55.890
Travel Guide

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:55.890
Going to the Edge of
\h\h\h\h\hEarth's Solar System

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:57.980
Take this with you.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:06:59.390
A travel guide?

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:00.730
Good thinking!

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:03.190
There are many famous spots
on the way to your destination,

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:05.610
so I thought you might want to enjoy the scenery.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:06.570
Let's see...

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:11.360
Go to Mercury, then from Venus to Mars,
and find... Ceres and Sedna?

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.370
Yes! Sedna's close right now!

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:22.630
When you find the fragment, call us immediately.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.630
If you call us from the heart, your voice
will reach us, no matter the distance.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:28.760
Sure.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:32.970
And then we'll all come flying to where you are.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:34.350
We'll be waiting for you.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.470
Good luck. We're rooting for you.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:39.600
I'm looking forward to it, too.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:41.350
Yeah.

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:44.900
I'll write letters.

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:46.270
They won't reach her...

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:47.730
Then I'll text.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:49.110
There aren't any transmitters out there.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:56.330
Nanako, are you sure
you don't mind going alone?

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:15.340
In the end, everyone is alone.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.240
Oh, right.

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:33.780
Going to the Edge of
Earth's Solar System

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:33.780
Travel Guide

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:48.000
I think that book said the scenery changes
as you approach the speed of light.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:51.590
When I get back, I'll have to tell Subaru.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:52.880
So, as you can you see,

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:57.720
when the problem includes two variables
to solve for, you also need two formulas.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:01.520
Where is Nanako right now?

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.600
Right around Neptune's orbit, I think.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:09.570
By 7:30 this evening, she should be
passing the first checkpoint, Sedna.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:13.860
Hey! President! You have your own!

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:11.030
She's already gone that far?

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.860
Do you think we could see her by telescope?

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:15.450
Huh?

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.490
Good evening!

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:21.660
Thanks for having us!

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:25.000
Welcome. Go on, have a look.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:26.750
Can you see her?

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:28.130
I can!

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:29.670
You found Nanako?

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:30.880
What color is she? Red?

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:33.090
There she is! I see her! She's red!

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:33.800
Liar!

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:35.380
Let me see, too!

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:38.930
Why are they looking at Sedna?

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:40.390
Quite the enthusiasts, aren't they?

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:48.270
You're eating a lot today...

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.650
Yeah! More, please!

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:50.650
Sure.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:52.400
Oh, by the way...

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:55.570
Was the blue all right?

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:58.030
Yeah! More, please!

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:03.040
These are the seeds you planted a week ago?

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.410
Yeah. They budded this morning.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.250
They'll bloom in three months.

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:09.670
Yeah!

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:14.170
A week has passed at one day per seven minutes...

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:16.840
I've passed Pioneer 10,

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:20.180
and now there's no one out here but me.

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:28.310
Okay, does anyone know how far this is?

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:29.140
Yes!

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:30.810
Oh, Nanako?

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:32.060
I don't know!

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:34.230
You should know this.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:35.610
Yes! I don't kno—

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:40.610
Nanako might be feeling sick.
I'll take her to the nurse's office.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:42.200
I'll answer, then!

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:44.290
I-Is this okay?

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.080
We have to see it through!

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:54.000
You think Nanako has made it there yet?

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:58.340
Without Nanako around, we can't
understand a word he says...

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:00.380
I hope we'll see her again soon.

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:07.470
They bloomed this morning.

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:24.740
<i>Even if I could see them,</i>

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:29.870
<i>they would blend with the light and
warmth of the Sun and the other stars.</i>

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.380
Nanako!

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.880
Come with me, too!

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:42.550
Your sister will catch up to us later.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:44.550
Really?

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:46.930
Really. Right?

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:48.770
Yeah, she will.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:56.360
<i>Adults always lie...</i>

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:02.030
<i>I knew that was a lie, too.</i>

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:11.540
<i>The day I keep that promise will never come.</i>

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:18.250
<i>Ever since then, my mother has taken
my brother around the world with her.</i>

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:21.880
<i>I haven't seen them once.</i>

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:26.050
<i>In the end, everyone is alone.</i>

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:31.480
<i>No matter who you love, or how much,
one day, you will be alone again.</i>

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:38.570
Oh?

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:52.790
<i>It feels like someone is calling me.</i>

00:12:58.250 --> 00:12:59.380
Oh, right.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:00.880
I need to call everyone.

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:07.340
I love stuff like this!

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:10.720
Just look at all the couples everywhere.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:12.770
I wish Nanako could've come with us.

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:15.020
Yeah, I wanted to see it with her.

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:20.940
Did you just hear something?

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:22.480
No, not really.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:24.490
Huh? That's odd...

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:32.160
They aren't coming.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:37.460
It's not reaching them because
I'm not calling from the heart.

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:42.090
After meeting the president and
becoming friends with all of them,

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:46.260
at some point, I grew accustomed
to being with others.

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:49.220
As though it had always been that way.

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:54.180
But I guess being alone suits me best, after all.

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:55.640
Maybe.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:57.140
Huh?

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:00.650
What is that?

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:04.900
Is that a planet?

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:10.410
I never imagined something like that
would be on the edge of our solar system.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:13.740
Have you been all alone?

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:19.920
Out here, where the Sun's light doesn't
reach, unnoticed by anyone, forever?

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.380
The fragment... I found it!

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:30.470
With this drive shaft, I can
capture the fragment on my own.

00:14:30.890 --> 00:14:32.680
I'll do it by myself!

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:37.430
<i>I shall give you a name.</i>

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:39.480
<i>A planet all alone...</i>

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:41.980
<i>Your name shall be Apate.</i>

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:44.360
<i>The goddess of fraud and despair.</i>

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:46.740
<i>Exactly what I am right now.</i>

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:56.250
I got it!

00:14:57.330 --> 00:14:59.460
This isn't good... It's dragging me.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:04.090
At this rate, I'll end up a
drifter instead of a Voyager.

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:09.090
The fragment!

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:26.690
I bet Subaru would be surprised to learn
there's a planet like this out here...

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:31.200
I wonder if it would be fun to
see with everyone together.

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.080
Nanako's calling us?

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.040
Yeah, I hear her.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.290
"There's a lonely..."

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:42.670
"...planet out here."

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:44.790
"Its name is..."

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:46.170
"...Apate."

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:47.590
Apate!

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:53.640
Huh?

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.050
Nanako!

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:57.680
It reached you.

00:15:57.680 --> 00:15:59.270
You're late contacting us!

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:01.850
Don't you know the rules
for associating with people?

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:03.190
Your hair's a mess.

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:04.900
Well done, Nanako!

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:07.480
So, this is Apate, then?

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:08.900
It's a planet?

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:11.530
Is it my imagination or is it bigger than Saturn?

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:14.530
From what I can tell, it's several
times larger than Jupiter.

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:18.450
I can't believe there's a planet out here
that no one ever knew about.

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:20.950
Thank you, Nanako.

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:22.920
No...

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:30.380
Is something wrong with the president?

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:33.380
He feels po-warm.

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:44.850
Is someone there?

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:45.900
Hello?

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:46.770
Hola?

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:47.770
Nihao?

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:48.270
Namaste?

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:51.360
Earth language is not necessary.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.950
I'm speaking directly to your mind.

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:56.740
Can you tell me

00:16:56.740 --> 00:17:00.330
how I appear to you?

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:02.040
You're...

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:10.250
Hey! Thank you for responding to my call!

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:13.050
That...

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:18.180
Pleiadian

00:17:18.350 --> 00:17:21.850
<i>One day I'll go with you to
meet an alien!</i>

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:25.230
A Pleiadian...

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:29.480
Oh? So that's what you imagined I'd look like?

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:38.370
Oh, that long-standing grudge is about to explode!

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:40.830
Even once you're alone,

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:44.000
your memories of being with
everyone never disappear.

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:46.330
The memories never go away.

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:47.750
Of course they don't!

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.090
It's been a while.

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:50.880
Hey.

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:52.250
Have you been well?

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:53.960
We waited three months!

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.800
Come on, let's hurry and finish up.

00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:58.390
So, where's the fragment?

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:00.140
Oh, over there.

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:03.220
Huh? That's it?

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:06.350
I see. So that's the fragment this time.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:09.020
When did you get here?

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:10.480
Did you hitchhike again?

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:12.520
I'd rather you called me a "free rider."

00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:14.440
H-Hey!

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:16.110
Hey! Wait!

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:18.570
We're going, too!

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:24.700
Oh, no!

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:29.290
I won't let you get away!

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:30.500
We have to go after him.

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:31.290
Wait!

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:34.500
That fragment might currently be operating.

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:37.170
Plus, it's flying toward that massive gas giant.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:39.340
A sea of liquid hydrogen lies at the
bottom of that hydrogen atmosphere,

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:42.050
and below that is a sea of metalized hydrogen
under five million atmospheres of pressure,

00:18:42.050 --> 00:18:43.100
and below that...

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:45.060
You look like a boiled octopus again!

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:48.440
This is a hydrogen sea?

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:54.190
What? Don't tell me that's...

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:56.860
After I've come this far, I have no choice.

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:04.490
Did something just shake?

00:19:04.490 --> 00:19:06.870
Space was just rocked by gravity waves.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:08.410
There's no mistake.

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:11.540
Gravitational compression is occurring
at the planet's center.

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:15.250
The engine fragment gathered energy
from the vacuum of space,

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:17.710
and it's causing the planet's
core to reach critical mass,

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:20.130
provoking a fusion reaction!

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:21.640
You're not making sense!

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:23.100
It's going to light up!

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:26.260
This planet is about to be reborn
into a fixed star, like your Sun!

00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:27.430
What?!

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:29.940
L-Look!

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:33.190
Everyone, grab on.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:44.240
Catch.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:47.040
It isn't hot?

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:48.080
I'm fine.

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:50.710
Hey, what's going to happen now?

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:53.540
Will we have a new fixed star in our solar system?

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:58.340
Yes. Soon, this will no longer be a planet,
but a companion star to your Sun.

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:02.680
Well, actually, binary star systems
are more common in space.

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:05.800
Then this planet isn't alone anymore?

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:08.270
I'm glad I got to see this with everyone.

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:14.860
It'll be three more months before this
star is observable from Earth.

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:22.280
The club room...

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:24.610
Huh? There's no snow!

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:26.370
What's going on?

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:27.370
Maybe...

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:30.370
We got sent back to the day Nanako left?

00:20:30.790 --> 00:20:33.870
From the start, there were two
possible moments of return.

00:20:33.870 --> 00:20:37.880
After the three months we spent,
or after the half a day Nanako spent.

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:41.760
So we're at a point half a day
after Nanako left?

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:47.890
One possibility is that someone wished
strongly to return to this moment...

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:49.510
We'll have to retake our tests.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:52.350
What a pain!

00:20:52.350 --> 00:20:55.020
Today was my birthday.

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:56.020
Huh?!

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.320
Oh, yeah! You're a Libra!

00:20:58.320 --> 00:20:59.650
We need to celebrate.

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:01.030
Is there anything you want?

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:04.150
Oh, I know!

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:08.450
When winter comes, we'll be able
to see beautiful lights in town.

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:09.780
Let's all go together!

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:11.330
There's the culture festival, too.

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:12.790
And Christmas.

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:14.210
Yeah.

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:26.130
Happy Birthday,
Sis

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:26.130
Congratulations,
Nanako

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:49.990
To Be Continued

00:21:57.170 --> 00:22:01.630
You see, when I looked up

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:07.010
everyone was smiling at me

00:22:07.010 --> 00:22:16.730
both embarrassed and happy as the starry sky turned sideways

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:25.690
Ever since then, whenever my daily life seemed hopeless

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:23.070
...my daily life...

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:35.450
I'd go into myself just for a little bit and ask

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:37.330
"Can I fly?"

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:41.540
"Sky" shall be our meeting point

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:47.170
We can go there whenever we want to

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:56.850
The wind sows stars in the far distance only light leaps to

00:22:56.850 --> 00:23:01.480
"We have to catch them!"

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:05.980
In the very moment I reached out my hand,

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:11.320
the scenery around us glittered

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:16.040
sometime after school

00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:23.590
The culture festival is coming up.

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:24.670
That reminds me...

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:29.050
We had a culture festival
after Nanako left, didn't we?

00:23:29.050 --> 00:23:30.050
Shh!

00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:32.680
Next episode: Planetarium Rendezvous.

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:33.760
Sob...
